# Target C — two-phase singleton-train capacity

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only conditional candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The two-phase outer-walk variation theorem gives a linear population of outer
occurrences whose directions are outside two selected bulk train directions.  This
note keeps those occurrences in the exact stopped-train ledger.  Under an explicit
two-phase bulk hypothesis, they remove trains from the bipartite crossing capacity of
the quadratic rhombus population.

The candidate chain is

```text
near-geodesic near-disk outer walk + two-phase quadratic bulk
 -> off-phase outer occurrences
 -> off-phase singleton/nonbulk trains
 -> smaller two-phase crossing capacity
 -> larger Q+4f and e_BB-k payment.
```

The final payment is still compatible with the current scalar endpoint region, so the
result does not close C-II.

## 1. Finite train classification

Let `G` be the occurrence-counted positive-contact plane graph, with strict
quadrilateral faces interpreted as rhombi.  Let `T` be its stopped-train count.  A
non-singleton stopped train contains a quadrilateral edge and has one fixed projective
direction: every edge in that train is parallel to the corresponding rhombus edges.
Singleton trains are exactly the contact edges incident to no quadrilateral face.

Fix two projective directions `[u]` and `[v]`.  Let `N_o` be the number of outer-face
edge occurrences whose projective directions are outside `{[u],[v]}`.  Let `T_o` be
the number of stopped trains whose projective direction is outside `{[u],[v]}`.

Every outer occurrence counted by `N_o` belongs to a train counted by `T_o`.  A stopped
train is a path and has at most two terminal outer-face occurrences, including the
singleton bridge convention.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{N_o\le 2T_o.}
\tag{ST.1}
\]

This is occurrence-safe.  It does not identify boundary vertices, and it includes
singleton trains, bridges, and repeated outer-face occurrences.

Now assume that all but `o(D^2)` strict-rhombus faces use the phase pair
`{[u],[v]}`.  Let `R_{uv}` be the number of such faces and let `T_u,T_v` count all
trains in the two selected projective directions.  Every `uv` rhombus is a crossing
of one `u` train and one `v` train.  The stopped-train crossing graph is simple, so

\[
R_{uv}\le T_uT_v
\le { (T-T_o)^2\over4}.
\tag{ST.2}
\]

The second inequality uses `T_u+T_v\le T-T_o`; off-phase trains cannot be used in
the two phase classes.  Singleton trains that happen to have phase direction only
make this upper bound weaker, which is safe.

Consequently,

\[
\boxed{
T\ge 2\sqrt{R_{uv}}+{N_o\over2}.
}
\tag{ST.3}
\]

This is the exact finite capacity inequality.  It is independent of any exceptional
face-to-boundary identification.

## 2. Insert the outer variation theorem

Assume along a Type-II subsequence that the occurrence-counted outer walk has

\[
\operatorname{width}_{Ju}(P_D)=D-o(D),
\qquad
\operatorname{width}_{Jv}(P_D)=D-o(D),
\]

and

\[
b\le \pi D+o(D).
\tag{ST.4}
\]

Write `s=sin(theta)`, where `theta` is the angle between the two selected unit
directions.  The finite determinant-variation inequalities give

\[
sN_u+N_o\ge2D-o(D),
\qquad
sN_v+N_o\ge2D-o(D).
\]

Since `b=N_u+N_v+N_o\le\pi D+o(D)`,

\[
N_o\ge {4-\pi s\over2-s}D-o(D).
\tag{ST.5}
\]

For `s\in[\sqrt3/2,1]`, the coefficient is decreasing in `s`, so

\[
\boxed{
N_o\ge(4-\pi)D-o(D).
}
\tag{ST.6}
\]

Combining (ST.3), (ST.6), and the Type-II strict-rhombus density

\[
{R_{uv}\over D^2}\longrightarrow
\Delta_2:={\pi\over2\sqrt3}
\]

gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T\over D}
\ge 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+2-{\pi\over2}
=2.3338292869\ldots .
}
\tag{ST.7}
\]

The exact hypothesis needed here is phase concentration of the **rhombus faces**:
`R_{uv}/D^2\to\Delta_2`.  A statement about train-direction measures alone would
not suffice, because one off-phase rhombus can introduce two off-phase trains.

## 3. Consequence for the canonical defect currency

The exact stopped-train ledger is

\[
2T=b+Q+4f.
\tag{ST.8}
\]

Set `B_D=(Q+4f)/D`.  Along a zero-detour subsequence, `b/D\to\pi`, so (ST.7) and
(ST.8) imply

\[
\boxed{
\liminf B_D
\ge
4\sqrt{\Delta_2}+4-2\pi
=1.5260659202\ldots .
}
\tag{ST.9}
\]

The exact Section-123 payment `Q+4f\le10(e_{BB}-k)` then yields the source-level
loaded-hull consequence

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {e_{BB}-k\over D}
\ge
{4\sqrt{\Delta_2}+4-2\pi\over10}
>0.1525.
}
\tag{ST.10}
\]

The zero-detour specialization hides a useful tradeoff. Let

\[
x:=\limsup {b\over D}
\]

along the same subsequence, and retain `s=sin(theta)` in the determinant estimate.
Adding the two variation inequalities gives

\[
s b+(2-s)N_o\ge4D-o(D),
\]

so

\[
{N_o\over D}
\ge {4-sx\over2-s}-o(1).
\tag{ST.11}
\]

The train ledger then yields

\[
{Q+4f\over D}
\ge
4\sqrt{\Delta_2}+{4-2x\over2-s}-o(1).
\tag{ST.12}
\]

Since `x>=pi>2` in the near-disk regime and
`s in [sqrt(3)/2,1]`, the right side is minimized at `s=1`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{Q+4f\over D}
\ge4\sqrt{\Delta_2}+4-2x-o(1).
}
\tag{ST.13}
\]

Writing `x=pi+delta` recovers (ST.9) at `delta=0` and records the exact loss
`2 delta` from outer-walk slack. This is the same canonical defect currency as
Section 123, but now coupled directly to the unresolved outer-detour quantity.

## 4. Why this still does not close C-II

The implication chain reaches an existing canonical currency, but the payment is only
linear.  The current normalized scalar region permits, for example, a point with
`t=(q-k)/D=2.9`, `k/D=0`, `Q/D=2.9-\pi/2`, and `f/D=0.4`; its value of
`(Q+4f)/D` is above `1.526`, and the other promoted scalar inequalities remain
feasible.  Thus (ST.9) does not force an exact unit triangle or a strict diameter
decrease.

The result identifies a sharper conditional target: prove that the actual Type-II
quadratic bulk is two-phase while simultaneously forcing an upper bound on
`e_{BB}-k`, or turn the resulting population of off-phase singleton/nonbulk trains
into a source-preserving descent.  The note does not assume either missing bridge.

## 5. Scope and classification

- All source points, including contact-isolated points, remain retained.
- The two-phase hypothesis is stated on rhombus faces, where the crossing-capacity
  argument is valid.
- The outer count is by physical edge occurrences, so bridges and articulation
  repetitions are retained.
- No identification of `N_o` with `P_exc` is made.
- Equation (ST.13) is a necessary tradeoff, not an upper bound on `x` or on the
  loaded/interface currency.
- No global-minimality conclusion is drawn from the scalar witness or the conditional
  capacity bound.

**Classification:** proved candidate under explicit two-phase/near-geodesic hypotheses;
proof-only and awaiting hostile audit. C-II remains open.
